Mockito — testing in isolation.

A J-unit test wants to check one class, but real classes lean on others. An order service calls a payment gateway, which calls a bank over the network. Test it as-is, and you're really testing the network — slow, flaky, and impossible to steer into the failure cases you actually care about. Mockito solves this by letting you replace a class's collaborators with test doubles — fake objects you control completely — so the test exercises your unit, and nothing else. This is the last tool, and the course's closing idea: isolation.

A mock is a stand-in that implements a dependency's interface, but does whatever you tell it. You create one, then stub its methods — script what they return — using when, then-return. So you create a fake payment gateway, and say: when it's asked to charge one hundred, return approved. Then you inject that fake into a real order service, and place an order. That exercises only the order service — no bank, no network. The gateway returns exactly what the test needs, instantly and deterministically. And you can script failure just as easily: say that any charge throws a timeout exception, and now you can test how the order service handles a dead payment provider, without ever having one.

Sometimes, though, the thing you need to check isn't a return value — it's a side effect. Did the unit call its collaborator correctly? Mockito records every call to a mock, and verify asserts that it happened. You can verify that the order service charged exactly one hundred. And you can verify that, on a failure, the emailer's send method was never called. This is how you test behavior that has no return value — that a failed order sends no confirmation, or that a retry fires exactly twice.

Two guardrails keep mocking honest. First: mock at the boundaries — the database, the network, the clock, the external service — not your own value objects. A record, or a simple domain class, should be used for real, not faked. Second: don't over-mock. A test that mocks everything ends up asserting that your code calls the methods you think it calls — verifying the implementation, rather than the behavior. It'll break on every refactor, while catching no real bugs. So mock the awkward collaborators, and use the real thing everywhere it's cheap. Mockito's own annotations cut the boilerplate once you lean on it.

And that completes the course. So step back, and see the whole arc. From your first main method, and the java virtual machine that runs it — through the object model, modern types and collections, generics, functional Java and streams, exceptions and input-output, concurrency, and finally the runtime, build, and testing that surround it all. The tools in this last module — the virtual machine's machinery, Maven or Gradle to build, J-unit and Mockito to verify — are what turn Java from a language you can write into software you can ship and trust. You now have the whole picture: not just how to write Java, but what runs it, what builds it, and how to prove it works. That's the foundation everything else — frameworks, libraries, whole ecosystems — is built on.
